## <%if .DisplayName%><%.DisplayName%><%else%><%.Slug%><%end%> — shape: a Verified Action (ADR-044).
##
## An agent prepares the work and commits; a tool then performs the one
## brittle ACTION with the quad `goal` + recipe + `postcondition` +
## `policy`, so it self-heals instead of hard-blocking: the postcondition
## is the truth at every rung (already met → skip; recipe → check;
## `recover` → an LLM proposes a corrected command from goal + stdout +
## stderr, re-run deterministically; still unmet → the policy decides).
## GATES stay deterministic: never attach recovery to a check whose
## recipe IS its postcondition (C105). The commit lands in a per-run git
## worktree — the TAG does not: git tags live in the repository's SHARED
## ref store, so it stays in your repo even when the run is discarded or
## its merge is skipped, and a second run with the same name meets the
## previous tag (the postcondition refuses it as stale, then recovery can
## only force-move it). Hence `tag` is REQUIRED and per run: an entry gate
## refuses an empty one before the agent runs. Fill in: the mission,
## `tag`. docs/adr/044-adaptive-recovery-for-deterministic-action-nodes.md
<%template "vars_block" .%>
schema check_out:
  configured: bool

schema prep_out:
  summary: string

schema tagged:
  tag: string

prompt mission:
<%indent 2 .Instructions%>

prompt prep_user:
  Prepare the release: apply the mission, update the changelog for the
  pending changes, and commit. Do NOT create the tag — the verified
  action after you does that.

## The entry gate, deterministic and before any LLM call: no tag name,
## no run. `!!` is the expression language's truthiness, which trims: an
## empty, blank or ABSENT tag is refused (so are the strings "false",
## "no" and "0", which no release is named).
compute check:
  output: check_out
  expr:
    configured: "!!vars.tag"

agent prepare:<%template "llm_fields" .%>
  system: mission
  user: prep_user
  output: prep_out

## The quad. The recipe may be brittle (the tag already exists, the
## working tree is dirty); the postcondition decides, and its JSON on
## stdout is the node's output on every rung, the skip included. It
## asserts the GOAL, not a weaker consequence: the object behind
## refs/tags/<tag> is a tag object (annotated — a lightweight tag is a
## bare ref and satisfies `^{commit}` alone) AND it points at HEAD — a
## tag that merely exists would let the idempotent-skip rung accept a
## stale one from an earlier run as success. A {{ref}} in a command is
## shell-escaped by the runtime, so it stays OUTSIDE quotes you write:
## here in the `$(…)` assignments, never inside the `"…"` tests (C137).
tool tag_release:
  command: `git tag -a {{vars.tag}} -m {{vars.tag}}`
  goal: "The annotated tag {{vars.tag}} exists on HEAD."
  postcondition: `t=$(git cat-file -t refs/tags/{{vars.tag}} 2>/dev/null); c=$(git rev-parse -q --verify refs/tags/{{vars.tag}}^{commit}); [ "$t" = tag ] && [ "$c" = "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" ] && printf '{"tag":"%s"}' {{vars.tag}}`
  output: tagged
  policy: recover
  recovery:
    max_repair_attempts: 2
    max_agent_attempts: 0

fail tag_unset:
  description: "no tag name was given"
  code: TAG_UNSET
  message: "tag is empty: name the annotated tag this run creates (a per-run name — the previous run's tag is still in the shared ref store)"

workflow <%.WorkflowName%>:
  entry: check
  ## The commit lands in a per-run git worktree — the tag does not (see
  ## the header): it is written to the shared ref store and outlives the
  ## worktree.
  worktree: auto<%template "dials" .%><%template "budget_block" .%>
  check -> tag_unset when not configured
  check -> prepare when configured
  prepare -> tag_release
  tag_release -> done
